    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0039" n="25" xml:id="pg25"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 Pacific War/3792</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Under Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Stettinius</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-23">February 23,
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I had a long talk this afternoon with Mr. John Davies<anchor n="71"
                        xml:id="d23-f1"/> and General Haydon Boatner.<anchor n="72" xml:id="d23-f2"
                    /> They presented a very discouraging story in connection with the relations
                    with the British and the Burma campaign, which the British are not taking
                    seriously.</p>
                <p>They also discussed our relations with the Chinese which are in many respects
                    unsatisfactory. General Stilwell is unable to deal with the situation locally so
                    sent his Chief of Staff to Washington to discuss it with the President and
                    General Marshall.<anchor n="73" xml:id="d23-f3"/>
                </p>
                <p>Owing to the vast importance of this matter and in order that we may have readily
                    available the basic information on your return, I have asked Joe Grew<anchor
                        n="74" xml:id="d23-f4"/> to meet with these two gentlemen tomorrow and get
                    the complete story which will be given to you on your return.</p>
                <p>Of course my first thought was that the story should be given to Dr.
                        Hornbeck,<anchor n="75" xml:id="d23-f5"/> but for reasons which I shall
                    explain to you personally, I have arranged for them to see Joe Grew.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>E[<hi rendition="#small-caps">dward</hi>] S[<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >tettinius</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d23-f1" n="71" xml:id="d23-f1-content">
                <p>John Paton Davies, Jr., Second Secretary of Embassy in China, temporarily in
                    Washington, on detail to General Stilwell’s staff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f2" n="72" xml:id="d23-f2-content">
                <p>Brig. Gen. H. L. Boatner, temporarily in Washington; Chief of Staff of the
                    Chinese Army in India and Burma.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f3" n="73" xml:id="d23-f3-content">
                <p>Gen. George C. Marshall, Chief of Staff, U. S. Army.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f4" n="74" xml:id="d23-f4-content">
                <p>Joseph C. Grew, Special Assistant to the Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d23-f5" n="75" xml:id="d23-f5-content">
                <p>Stanley K. Hornbeck, Director of the Office of Far Eastern Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
